{"id": "sevt_01DoYpTnTeqPVWxKcLS6dhYn", "content": [{"text": "**Root cause:** `371dfe2` made `build_summary` run one query per order row instead of one batched query. The extra queries drove the checkout DB pool to saturation. The cache-disable commit `db46df5` was not the cause.\n\n**Evidence**\n- **Deploys:** `371dfe2` (checkout, 14:06) is the first deploy to line up with the latency rise. The only deploys before it are `e0a27f7` on inventory at 13:02 and nothing else on checkout. p99 was about 75ms until 14:05. At 14:06 it began climbing steadily: 101ms at 14:06, 294ms at 14:19, 412ms at 14:28.\n- **Diff:** The refactor replaced a single `order_id__in` query with a per-order `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=...)` inside a loop. That is the N+1 pattern from the runbook, labelled \"readability\".\n- **Pool:** `db_pool_utilization` for checkout rose from about 0.14 at 14:06 to 0.71 at 14:28. It passed 0.75 at 14:29 and hit 1.0 at 14:38, where it stayed.\n- **Log:** `grep` found 3,793 per-row `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` lines. The per-10-minute counts are:\n\n  | Window | Per-row queries |\n  |---|---|\n  | 14:00 | 128 |\n  | 14:10 | 353 |\n  | 14:20 | 319 |\n  | 14:30 | 873 |\n  | 14:40 | 1142 |\n\n  The 14:00 window starts before the 14:06 deploy, so it includes pre-deploy and post-deploy time.\n- **Cache change:** `db46df5` disabled the catalog cache at 14:18 and was rolled back at 14:23. Latency kept climbing through the rollback, so it was at most a minor contributor.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The jump to about 2.1s at 14:29 and about 4s by 14:31 doesn't match any deploy. It probably happened when the pool crossed roughly 0.75 utilization and requests started queueing. I haven't confirmed that threshold, and the pool didn't reach 1.0 until 14:38. The log showed only minor GC pauses around 14:29\u201314:30, with no pool or timeout errors.\n- The 14:06 start is based on the metric and the deploy timestamp. I didn't check which hosts picked up `371dfe2` first.\n\n**Fix:** Revert `371dfe2`. If the readability refactor is still wanted, keep the batched query and group the results in memory.\n\n> **Root cause:** `371dfe2` \u2014 the order-summary refactor replaced one batched `order_id__in` query with a per-order query loop (N+1). That exhausted the checkout DB pool and pushed p99 from about 75ms to over 4s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:53:13.818443Z", "type": "agent.message"}
